The accounts
administrator-01-accounts-and-security
The list is every registered account but SDSYS: its kind (user, group or other), whether it is suspended, the gates that are on for it and how many sessions are signed on in it. The box at the foot shows the account under the cursor: its path, the Windows user and group and the routes as the register and the Windows groups give them, a group account's members, the gates that are on and who is signed on in it. The gate switches themselves are on the gates page; a new account has every gate off.
S suspends the account under the cursor or lifts its suspension: LOGIN, ssh and the API refuse a suspended account, nothing changes in Windows, and lifting it costs nothing. A and R add a Windows user to the group of a group account or take one away. C creates an account: a user account asks for the new person's password on the verb's own prompt, which shows nothing and which no part of this page sees; an other account's directory is written with forward slashes (C:/Data). X deletes an account after you type its name; DELETE.ACCOUNT then asks once on the terminal, and there is no taking it back.
Every key shows the command it will run and what it does and runs it only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. The verbs ask for the administrator again and write their own audit lines. F5 reads the register, the groups and the sessions again. The letters are this page's commands, so / is how you find an account by typing: it goes to the next one whose name holds the text.
